  "query_preview": "..A persistent, positively tilted upper-level low will\nlinger over the region through the end of the week and into the \nweekend. As an upper-level ridge over the Alaska Peninsula tracks\neastward, this trough will evolve into a closed-off low before \nweakening and moving out of the region by Sunday. Simultaneously, \na surface low originating from Prince William Sound will stall \njust west of Haida Gwaii, gradually weakening as its upper-level \nsupport wanes. This pattern will result in bands of rain pushing \nnorthward, primarily impacting the southern Panhandle and Coast \nRange, though precipitation totals are expected to remain \nseasonably normal, with the highest QPF amounts on Wednesday and \npossibly Friday. While the southern Panhandle remains \npredominantly wet and showery under persistent SE'rly flow, the \nNorth Gulf Coast is expected to see a break in heavier \nrain showers towards the end of the week. As the surface low \ndissipates southward over the weekend and a ridge attempts to \nbuild, northerly winds and warming 850mb temperatures over the \nnorthern and inland parts of the panhandle may lead to down \nsloping and warmer surface temperatures in the mid 70s near \nYakutat, Skagway, Haines, and Hyder. Model guidance is in general\nagreement regarding the evolution of the low, though there is \nsome uncertainty in resolving local wind convergence through Dixon\nEntrance into southern Clarence Strait. While winds will likely \nsee their most significant push northward on Wednesday, current \nthinking is that wind speeds will remain around 20kts or less, \ngradually diminishing afterwards.",
